F</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Idiopathic splenic vein stenosis: a cause of gastric variceal haemorrhage</article-title><source><italic>Southern Medical Journal</italic></source><year>2000</year><volume>93</volume><issue>8</issue><fpage>812</fpage><lpage>814</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="other">2-s2.0-0034241546</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10963517</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B7"><label>7</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Srinivasan</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Polycystic liver disease: an unusual cause of bleeding varices</article-title><source><italic>Digestive Diseases and Sciences</italic></source><year>1999</year><volume>44</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>389</fpage><lpage>392</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="other">2-s2.0-0032956909</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10063928</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="fig1" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Perforation showing free air in the peritoneum in the area of splenic flexure.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.SURGERY2013-695318.001"/></fig><fig id="fig2" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Cluster of varices in the left upper quadrant medial to the splenic hilum with multiple gastric varices within the gastric lumen.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.SURGERY2013-695318.002"/></fig></floats-group></article>